1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,483
A pine cone on a hill trip.

2
00:00:02,483 --> 00:00:04,354
A cycas in the city park.

3
00:00:04,354 --> 00:00:06,779
Tall pines with leaves like needles.

4
00:00:07,179 --> 00:00:08,965
All three are gymnosperms.

5
00:00:08,965 --> 00:00:11,889
They make seeds, but never inside a fruit.

6
00:00:12,339 --> 00:00:15,506
Aarav picked up a pine cone
on a hill station trip.

7
00:00:15,506 --> 00:00:19,323
He shook it, and small seeds
fell out from between its scales.

8
00:00:19,323 --> 00:00:22,527
He asked: why is there no
fruit around these seeds?

9
00:00:22,877 --> 00:00:24,389
Gymnos means naked.

10
00:00:24,389 --> 00:00:25,847
Sperma means seed.

11
00:00:25,847 --> 00:00:28,993
Gymnosperms are the
plants with naked seeds.

12
00:00:29,493 --> 00:00:33,779
By the end, you will know the body
of a gymnosperm, how it makes seeds,

13
00:00:33,779 --> 00:00:35,851
and how to tell Pinus from Cycas.

14
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:38,791
So what does a gymnosperm look like?

15
00:00:39,141 --> 00:00:41,944
Most are medium or tall trees, or shrubs.

16
00:00:41,944 --> 00:00:46,645
The giant redwood, Sequoia, is one
of the tallest tree species on Earth.

17
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:49,291
The roots are usually tap roots.

18
00:00:49,291 --> 00:00:51,646
Pinus roots team up with a fungus.

19
00:00:51,646 --> 00:00:53,425
This is called mycorrhiza.

20
00:00:53,875 --> 00:00:57,653
Cycas has small special
roots called coralloid roots.

21
00:00:57,653 --> 00:01:01,085
Cyanobacteria live inside
them and fix nitrogen.

22
00:01:01,735 --> 00:01:05,073
Now let's zoom into a pine
tree, one level at a time.

23
00:01:05,423 --> 00:01:08,904
The tree has a branched
stem and needle like leaves.

24
00:01:08,904 --> 00:01:13,676
It carries two kinds of cones: small
male cones and bigger female cones.

25
00:01:14,176 --> 00:01:15,076
Zoom in.

26
00:01:15,015 --> 00:01:19,002
The female cone is made of
scales arranged in a spiral.

27
00:01:19,002 --> 00:01:21,289
Each scale is a megasporophyll.

28
00:01:21,789 --> 00:01:22,748
Zoom again.

29
00:01:22,748 --> 00:01:25,331
On top of one scale sit two ovules.

30
00:01:25,331 --> 00:01:26,820
Nothing covers them.

31
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:28,049
They are naked.

32
00:01:28,549 --> 00:01:30,080
Zoom into one ovule.

33
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:33,346
The nucellus is wrapped
in a protective envelope.

34
00:01:33,346 --> 00:01:38,092
Inside it, the female gametophyte
holds two or more archegonia, each with

35
00:01:38,092 --> 00:01:38,992
an egg.

36
00:01:39,481 --> 00:01:41,860
How does a gymnosperm make a seed?

37
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:43,617
Watch it in five stages.

38
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:44,932
Stage one.

39
00:01:44,932 --> 00:01:47,027
Male cones make microspores.

40
00:01:47,027 --> 00:01:52,627
Each grows into a pollen grain, a male
gametophyte made of only a few cells.

41
00:01:53,377 --> 00:01:54,340
Stage two.

42
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:59,910
In the ovule, a megaspore mother cell
divides by meiosis into four megaspores.

43
00:01:59,910 --> 00:02:02,690
One grows into the female gametophyte.

44
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:04,451
Stage three.

45
00:02:04,451 --> 00:02:08,905
Air currents carry the pollen
grains to the opening of the ovules.

46
00:02:08,905 --> 00:02:10,397
This is pollination.

47
00:02:11,147 --> 00:02:12,137
Stage four.

48
00:02:12,137 --> 00:02:16,840
A pollen tube grows towards the
archegonia and releases the male gametes

49
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:17,776
near them.

50
00:02:17,776 --> 00:02:19,605
One fuses with the egg.

51
00:02:20,355 --> 00:02:21,348
Stage five.

52
00:02:21,348 --> 00:02:25,849
The zygote grows into an embryo,
and the ovule becomes a seed.

53
00:02:25,849 --> 00:02:28,073
No ovary wall, so no fruit.

54
00:02:28,073 --> 00:02:29,735
The seed stays naked.

55
00:02:30,485 --> 00:02:31,496
Remember this.

56
00:02:31,496 --> 00:02:35,029
In gymnosperms, the gametophytes
never live on their own.

57
00:02:35,029 --> 00:02:38,175
They stay inside the
sporangia, on the sporophyte.

58
00:02:38,775 --> 00:02:42,099
Why do pines survive
cold, dry and windy hills?

59
00:02:42,099 --> 00:02:44,838
Their leaves explain it, step by step.

60
00:02:45,188 --> 00:02:46,088
Step one.

61
00:02:46,002 --> 00:02:49,880
A needle shape has a small
surface, so less water escapes.

62
00:02:50,630 --> 00:02:51,530
Step two.

63
00:02:51,455 --> 00:02:55,916
A thick cuticle, a waxy coat on
the outside, holds the water in.

64
00:02:56,666 --> 00:02:57,607
Step three.

65
00:02:57,607 --> 00:03:01,074
The stomata, the tiny
pores, sit sunken in pits.

66
00:03:01,074 --> 00:03:04,183
Dry wind cannot pull
the water out so easily.

67
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:07,915
Now let's put Pinus
and Cycas side by side.

68
00:03:08,265 --> 00:03:12,269
Pinus: a branched stem, needle
leaves, and roots with a fungus.

69
00:03:12,269 --> 00:03:15,014
Male and female cones
grow on the same tree.

70
00:03:15,464 --> 00:03:20,276
Cycas: a stem with no branches, big
compound leaves like feathers, and

71
00:03:20,276 --> 00:03:21,508
coralloid roots.

72
00:03:21,508 --> 00:03:25,172
Male cones and megasporophylls
grow on different trees.

73
00:03:25,772 --> 00:03:26,718
Your turn.

74
00:03:26,718 --> 00:03:31,354
Male cones grow on one tree, and
megasporophylls on another tree.

75
00:03:31,354 --> 00:03:32,638
Pinus or Cycas?

76
00:03:32,638 --> 00:03:34,031
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:37,231 --> 00:03:38,131
Cycas!

78
00:03:37,889 --> 00:03:42,163
In Cycas, the male and female
parts grow on separate trees.

79
00:03:42,813 --> 00:03:44,510
Now, a worked example.

80
00:03:44,510 --> 00:03:46,308
Pinus, Cycas, or both?

81
00:03:46,308 --> 00:03:47,955
Let's sort six features.

82
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:51,379
Needle leaves and
mycorrhiza belong to Pinus.

83
00:03:51,829 --> 00:03:55,536
Coralloid roots and an
unbranched stem belong to Cycas.

84
00:03:55,986 --> 00:03:59,123
Naked seeds and pollen
grains belong to both.

85
00:03:59,123 --> 00:04:01,007
Every gymnosperm has them.

86
00:04:01,657 --> 00:04:03,123
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:03,473 --> 00:04:06,470
Many students call the
pollen grain a male gamete.

88
00:04:06,870 --> 00:04:08,358
It is not a gamete.

89
00:04:08,358 --> 00:04:11,264
The pollen grain is a
tiny male gametophyte.

90
00:04:11,264 --> 00:04:14,021
It carries the male gametes to the ovule.

91
00:04:14,671 --> 00:04:16,038
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:16,038 --> 00:04:20,573
As match the following, and as one
line facts on roots, cones and pollen.

93
00:04:21,173 --> 00:04:22,392
Exam level now.

94
00:04:22,392 --> 00:04:24,727
Match each item with its feature.

95
00:04:24,727 --> 00:04:25,997
Pause and solve.

96
00:04:31,397 --> 00:04:32,773
The answer is B.

97
00:04:33,273 --> 00:04:36,706
Coralloid roots are in
Cycas, with cyanobacteria.

98
00:04:36,706 --> 00:04:39,553
Mycorrhiza is in Pinus, with a fungus.

99
00:04:40,053 --> 00:04:42,525
Sequoia is one of the tallest trees.

100
00:04:42,525 --> 00:04:45,769
And a pollen grain is a
reduced male gametophyte.

101
00:04:46,369 --> 00:04:47,829
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:47,829 --> 00:04:49,331
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:52,531 --> 00:04:55,367
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:04:55,767 --> 00:04:56,667
One.

105
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,923
Why are gymnosperm seeds called naked?

106
00:04:59,823 --> 00:05:00,723
Two.

107
00:05:00,397 --> 00:05:03,864
What lives inside the
coralloid roots of Cycas?

108
00:05:04,764 --> 00:05:05,664
Three.

109
00:05:05,386 --> 00:05:07,588
How do pine needles save water?

110
00:05:08,688 --> 00:05:11,767
Next time: a grass blade and a mango leaf.

111
00:05:11,767 --> 00:05:14,095
Why are their lines so different?

112
00:05:14,095 --> 00:05:17,421
We find out in angiosperms,
monocots and dicots.
